riscv: dts: sophgo: sg2000: milkv-duos: enable I2C3 + camera-0 pinmux

Milestone 0 of MIPI CSI camera support on the Milk-V Duo S: bring up the
GC2083 camera's *control plane* so the sensor is reachable from user
space (i2cdetect -y 3 -> 0x37). The MIPI D-PHY data lanes and the capture
pipeline are out of scope -- this 6.12 tree has no CV1800B CSI-2 receiver
or ISP driver, and none is upstream yet -- but the I2C bus and the pad mux
added here are reused verbatim by whatever camera stack lands later (a
mainline V4L2 sensor subdev, or a forward-port of the vendor cif bridge),
because the pin assignment is a fixed board fact either way.

The J1 FPC connector on the Duo S wires the camera to I2C3 (SCL/SDA),
CAM_MCLK0 (sensor master clock) and CAM_RST0 (SENSOR_RSTN0, muxed to
GPIO porta 2). The SoC I2C controllers already exist in cv18xx.dtsi
(snps,designware-i2c) but ship status = "disabled"; i2c3 is enabled here
and given the I2C3 pin group. The pin groups extend the SG2000 pin
controller instantiated for the eMMC (patch 0023). Pads and mux values
are taken from the vendor board file:

  build/boards/cv181x/sg2000_milkv_duos_.../u-boot/cvi_board_init.c
    PINMUX_CONFIG(IIC3_SCL, IIC3_SCL);    -> func 0
    PINMUX_CONFIG(IIC3_SDA, IIC3_SDA);    -> func 0
    PINMUX_CONFIG(CAM_MCLK0, CAM_MCLK0);  -> func 0
    PINMUX_CONFIG(CAM_RST0, XGPIOA_2);    -> func 3 (GPIO porta 2)

All four pads are on the 1.8 V VDD18A_MIPI domain, so power-source =
<1800> (mandatory for every group in this pinctrl driver). Like the
vendor we only mux, without setting internal bias: the board carries
external 4.7k I2C3 pull-ups to 1.8 V. An i2c3 alias pins the bus to
/dev/i2c-3 (matching the connector name and the vendor user-space's
hard-coded bus number) instead of a dynamic index.

Each group MUST wrap its pinmux/power-source in a child sub-node: this
pinctrl driver's dt_node_to_map walks the child nodes of the referenced
group (for_each_available_child_of_node), exactly like the eMMC group in
patch 0023. A flat group produces zero maps -- the pads are silently left
at their reset default and the controller logs "there is not valid maps
for state default".

The MCLK and reset pads are muxed here (a permanent board fact) but only
*routed*: the CAM0 clock stays gated and the reset line is not driven --
enabling MCLK and releasing reset are inherently camera-driver
responsibilities and are done, for bench testing only, in the separate
patch 0025.

Needs CONFIG_I2C_DESIGNWARE_PLATFORM (+ _CORE) and CONFIG_I2C_CHARDEV in
config-6.12 (replacing the unused CONFIG_I2C_OCORES).

Signed-off-by: d622 <300083818+d622-au@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duos.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duos.dts
@@ -131,3 +131,75 @@
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 };
+
+&{/aliases} {
+	i2c3 = &i2c3;
+};
+
+/*
+ * Camera 0 / J1 CSI connector (CAM-GC2083) -- Milestone 0: sensor control
+ * plane only. Bring up I2C3 plus the sensor's MCLK and reset pads so the
+ * GC2083 is reachable from user space:
+ *
+ *   i2cdetect -y 3   ->   device at 0x37
+ *
+ * The MIPI D-PHY data lanes and any capture pipeline are deliberately out of
+ * scope: this 6.12 tree has no in-tree CV1800B CSI-2 receiver / ISP driver,
+ * and none is upstream. This patch is the part that is reused verbatim by
+ * whatever camera stack lands later -- a mainline V4L2 sensor subdev, or a
+ * forward-port of the vendor cif bridge -- because the pad mux is a fixed
+ * board fact either way.
+ *
+ * Pads + mux values are taken from the vendor board file
+ *   build/boards/cv181x/sg2000_milkv_duos_.../u-boot/cvi_board_init.c:
+ *     PINMUX_CONFIG(IIC3_SCL, IIC3_SCL);   -> I2C3 SCL (func 0)
+ *     PINMUX_CONFIG(IIC3_SDA, IIC3_SDA);   -> I2C3 SDA (func 0)
+ *     PINMUX_CONFIG(CAM_MCLK0, CAM_MCLK0); -> sensor MCLK (func 0)
+ *     PINMUX_CONFIG(CAM_RST0, XGPIOA_2);   -> GPIO porta 2 == SENSOR_RSTN0 (func 3)
+ * All four pads are on the 1.8 V VDD18A_MIPI domain, so power-source = <1800>.
+ * Like the vendor we only mux (no bias): I2C3 has external 4.7k pull-ups to
+ * 1.8 V on-board.
+ *
+ * NOTE the two-level group structure: this pinctrl driver's dt_node_to_map
+ * walks the *child* nodes of each referenced node (for_each_available_child_
+ * of_node), so every group label must wrap its pinmux/power-source in a
+ * sub-node -- exactly like the eMMC group in patch 0023. A flat group yields
+ * zero maps ("i2c_designware ...: there is not valid maps for state default")
+ * and the pads are silently left at their reset default.
+ *
+ * i2c3 owns the pin groups only because it is the sole probing node here; that
+ * just leaves the MCLK pad routed to the (still-gated) CAM0 clock and the
+ * reset pad in GPIO mode -- both harmless. The *dynamic* half (turning MCLK on,
+ * driving reset) is inherently driver-owned and lives in the separate bring-up
+ * patch 0025 for bench testing.
+ */
+&{/soc/pinctrl@3001000} {
+	i2c3_pins: i2c3-pins {
+		i2c3-grp {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_IIC3_SCL, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_IIC3_SDA, 0)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	cam0_mclk_pins: cam0-mclk-pins {
+		cam0-mclk-grp {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_CAM_MCLK0, 0)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	cam0_rst_pins: cam0-rst-pins {
+		cam0-rst-grp {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_CAM_RST0, 3)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&i2c3 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c3_pins &cam0_mclk_pins &cam0_rst_pins>;
+	clock-frequency = <100000>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
